welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Colonel Timothy McAteer a military  postgraduate fellow at Yale University  he's here on a year-long appointment to  the Jackson Institute for Global Affairs  Colonel McAteer is an active-duty  US Army infantry officer with 23 years  of service the majority of his service  has been as a paratrooper with the 82nd  Airborne Division he has multiple  tactical and operational level  deployments ranging from rifle platoon  leader in Desert shield/desert storm and  the Sinai Egypt up through service as a  battalion and division operations  officer and command at the battalion  level in Afghanistan and the brigade  level in Haiti and Iraq today we talked  with Colonel McAteer about his role as a  military fellow at Yale welcome colonel  McAteer Thank You Marilyn it's great to  be here today so let's begin with an  overview of the program tell us about it  and how and how you became interested in  it well I'll start with a quick overview  of the Army's professional military  education program which the fellowship  falls into at the later stages for  officers you start with  pre-commissioning training about your  fifth year in the Army you get pulled  out of the active force go into a  schoolhouse institution environment and  that's where you learn about staff  functions and company level command  halfway through your career in the army  about your tenth or eleventh year as a  major you'll go to the command and  General Staff College which is higher  level staffs and then the last stage  around 1920 years you go to what is  known as Senior Service College the  fellowship is in lieu of the Senior  Service College so I will get senior  service college credit by being a fellow  here at Yale most of my  coworkers or other officers in the army  and senior service college or at one of  the various service war colleges that  said though there are about 90 army  fellows throughout the nation right now  in colleges and think tanks and then  some informed foreign nations at their  staff colleges we have fellows at  Harvard MIT Georgetown and University of  Texas we have fellows at the Brookings  Institute CSIS the center for new  American security down in DC and then we  have fellows in Pakistan and France and  the UK so particularly though for here  Yale the fellowship program here as you  stated earlier is it Jackson that's  where my one year appointment is and  that program is one year it's non-degree  producing it gives me credit for the  senior service college and my role in  Jackson is - well I attend an audit  courses that I that I am interested in  and I also focus my studies in the in  the global affairs program the grant  strategy program international relations  program kind of my interest and that  keeps me tied in with the War College  I've been assigned a faculty mentor at  Jack Jackson because I also have  research and writing requirements that I  will send back to the War College and  they'll kind of check off and say okay  you've completed your requirements for  research of a topic that is of interest  and importance to the army what is that  well right now I'm still in the process  of developing a research question but  I'm focusing on Middle East North Africa  I think and some of our policies there  as you look around today when the events  in in Libya and then in northern Africa  after the Arab Spring and how they  relate to our foreign policy and  recently in the last couple years the  military's established Africa Command  which is a regional geographic combatant  command focused on the continent so  they're all relevant topics as we look  at the military instrument of power  being employed in northern Africa so  that's what I'm looking at okay and why  did you choose to come to Yale versus  doing the service school program well a  lot of reasons you have to compete first  of all so you put in a list of  preferences and kind of cross your  fingers I leaned heavy towards going to  a college university setting for a  couple reasons  personally I felt it was important at  this stage of my career to maybe not  that the army or the service schools are  not helpful but to get away from an  environment where I'm very comfortable  and be immersed into an education  environment that takes me out of my  comfort zone  and I think Yale has already started to  do that in many ways also the access to  the intellectual capital that an  institution like Yale has is unequaled  anywhere in the United States the  program here you know is only two years  old and I might have mentioned that  before I'm only the second fellow here  and and it's interesting the way the  culture is changing a little bit Yale  with respect to the military right we  just allowed the ROTC to come back on  campus  I believe maybe a year or two ago and  I'm not quite sure of the timing but  fairly recently right yeah both Air  Force and Navy have programs here a  little disappointed that the army didn't  one of the things I'm not here to do  though Yale is to recruit and try to get  people into the military I'm here to  that's more of a personal enlightenment  kind of it is it is and then I also  believe that I have something to  contribute to the classroom as well  right let's talk about that a little bit  what do you think that you can  contribute well I think I bring a little  bit different perspective on on some  global affairs from a very practical  experience level I've been deployed  multiple times at various levels from  the tactical operational a strategic  level you know this training program the  the War College or Senior Service  College fellowship is designed to get me  to think at the strategic level so I'm  learning a lot in that but as I've  learned in a lot of the class  this grant strategy and some of the  international relation classes that I  attend there's a lot of interest in Iraq  and Afghanistan and how the military as  an instrument of national power is  employed and I feel like I can give some  real-world practical experiences to  contribute to the dialogue within the  classroom can you think of one  particular example of where you actually  contributed something that you  experienced I was able in one of the  great programs here is the grant  strategy program with Professor Gatos  professor Hill professor Kennedy and I  have the great honour of receiving with  a panel including those gentlemen  marshal briefs from from the students  and these marshal briefs our grand  strategy briefs that they've developed  and then they present them to us and I  always play a senior DoD official so I  can give the Department of Defense's  position on their strategy towards let's  say the Middle East I just said through  one that was the revitalizing Europe and  the relationship of the United States in  Europe and and the defence structure in  Europe so what I was able to offer when  we talked about the particulars of that  student-led proposal was the the  concerns of the military in terms of  basing in terms of reduction of forces  and places that you know we might feel  are a little more important where they  had had through their readings thought  well this would be very easy to do and I  was able to give them I think a dose of  reality in the application of their  strategy prior to you coming to this  program I know your active duty where  were you stationed and what were you  doing for the last ten years I've been  at Fort Bragg and for the last ten years  I've been very involved in well the two  wars that we've been involved with I've  been engaged with I started out coming  out of that mid-level school that I  mentioned earlier the command and  General Staff College and I was able to  attend a second year at Fort Leavenworth  we like to call Fort Leavenworth the  intellectual center of the army  they're in Kansas but it is and that's  our schoolhouse at the advanced military  Studies program which gave me access to  jobs that it might not have gotten  before and I became a planner at the  division level as we were going into  Iraq in 2003 so that gave me a lot of  exposure it's a lot of hard work but  that gave me a lot of exposure into how  operations are really put together on a  grand scale and from that job I served  in a battalion level which is an  infantry battalion about 800  paratroopers in the 82nd and deployed to  Afghanistan deployed again to Iraq came  back and then I just kind of worked my  way up through the division I ended up  commanding a battalion in Afghanistan  for 15 months doing primarily  counterinsurgency operations in two  pretty large provinces inside of  Afghanistan and what does that actually  mean counterinsurgency what do you do  well counterinsurgency is it's an  interesting operational concept and  there's a lot of debate in the military  but it has proven to be fairly effective  when dealing in post-conflict type  environments when we finished in early  2001 2002 the Taliban were defeated in  Afghanistan and you know was this influx  of more conventional forces to try to  consolidate the gains and then the  mission as you know in Iraq in both  Afghanistan they started to evolve a  counter insurgency has a component of it  which could be considered  nation-building but it's a it's a an  operation that is very population  century where you you were trying to  build the security forces and the  capability security forces within the  nation the host nation so you're working  with them to help them do that  absolutely so we do partnered operations  with their military with their police  and but the centerpiece of the strategy  is the population is protecting the  population from insurgents who are  trying to destabilize the situation it's  extremely complex  a times be very dangerous but it can  also be very very rewarding when you  start to see the progress in security  forces when you start to see local  economies and the populations starting  to flourish when you start to see  schools open children girls in the in  those nations in particular starting to  get an opportunity for an education in  some some places but it's very difficult  and then the goal always a  counterinsurgencies to eventually turn  over operations to the nation and then  depart it takes a very long time and  there's a point in counterinsurgency  it's a crossover point where their  security forces become capable enough to  do independent operations not just  tactically but also operationally  through logistics and command and  control primarily so they can control  and sustain their forces and that takes  a very long time and then my last job  was as a brigade commander I took  command in Haiti right after the  earthquake the brigade had deployed  there I left Afghanistan  showed up in Haiti took command I was  there for a couple of months and that  was extremely rewarding rewarding  operation and then back to Fort Bragg  and then we were the last Brigade in  Iraq that operated in Anbar and Baghdad  provinces and then we redeployed back in  December so this time last year I was in  in Iraq came home in late December and  then prepared to come to Yale so what  are you hoping to ultimately take away  from your experience here yeah what I've  discovered you know you asked earlier  what what I bring to the program and I  think diversity is probably the one word  that would sum up what this experience I  think I bring a diverse set of ideas and  views to the institution but more  importantly and what I think I'll take  away from here is I've been exposed to  very diverse and different points of  view often from some very young and  intelligent extremely intelligent and  driven students here yeah I'm very  impressed by the students and the  faculty but primarily  undergrad and graduate students that  I've been in contact with Yale will  produce and has produced and will  continue to produce senior policy  officials within our nation  I think if I can impart on them a little  bit of a better understanding on the  military instrument of national power in  my short time here and then they can  impart on me  a different way of looking at the same  problems and broaden my horizons a  little bit broaden my understanding from  a civilian perspective I think that's  very important because ultimately the  military rightly so I mean we work for  our civilian leaders day in and day out  so that dialogue is critically I know  you've been here only a short time but  in that time have you have any of your  views been changed on on anything that  you you know had previously thought no  III I think I'm I'm learning to be a  little more of a critical thinker you  know over the last ten years we've  worked out of our comfort zone a lot a  lot of interagency work a lot of work  with the State Department with  nongovernmental organizations that that  five or six years ago would have been  almost unheard of with military and and  the drivers of that or really our our  junior officers and our noncommissioned  officers our 20s you know mid 20 or 30  level leadership in the army  I was on a little bit of the tail end of  that but you quickly learned how  important it is that we we do our best  to speak with one voice we have unity of  effort in our operations but interagency  employment of all the instruments of  national power are critically important  and so you have a good environment here  to develop those skills so a particular  case probably not but I definitely you  know you you can't help but sit in some  of these classes with with the the  professor's that are here and and not  get a you sit in awesome times at their  level of understanding and the depth of  their understand  on the topics that they discussed  whether it's national strategy or or  economics or the study of social theory  and political science and and it it's  forced me to be a lot more I think  critical of my thinking maybe a little  more skeptical of some of the things  that I've heard outside of the military  so in that regard has been very helpful  and what are your plans once you  complete your fellowship here it's hard  to tell I'll go back into the active  force I mean I'm still active yes but  I'll go back to work one of the  beautiful things here is you know six  months ago I was responsible for 3,700  paratroopers in a brigade with six  battalions Wow and now I'm responsible  for myself it's humbling sometimes when  you're when you command an organization  at large - - huge number of people it is  and and and when you're the commander  that you know you kind of people tell  you what you want here and they they  give you the things that you you want  when you ask for me but here I have none  of that  so it's humbling at times and it's it's  important to remember that but I will go  from here and go back into the active  force I could potentially end up on the  Joint Staff down in DC I could go to one  of the combatant commands either PACOM  out in the Pacific or AFRICOM which is  responsible for Africa but headquartered  in Germany right now or potentially I  could go back to Afghanistan now do you  get to do any to have a voice in where  you go or basically are just sent to  where they tell you to go very little  voice needs the of the army come first  they work with us but at the end of the  day we go where we're told to go and and  serve to the best of our ability  wherever we end up okay well this has  been very enlightening thank you so much  for being here today and sharing some of  your work well I appreciate the  opportunity if I could just to finish  this all right  the one thing I'd like to add is is you  know I'll be followed by another fellow  next year we are available resources for  anybody that wants to bring us into  discussion lectures panels  and not to just promote a military  standpoint but to offer a military stash  another viewpoint another viewpoint and  so we're here and we're willing to help  in any way possible and participate in  any way possible good to know thank you  very much for being here Thank You  Marilyn okay for more information about  colonel McAteer please visit our website  at Yale edu backslash Macmillan report  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

